  "strategy": "### Approach and Rationale\nThe Erds minimum overlap problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap integral \\( C_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k))  dx \\) for a step function \\( h(x) \\) defined on [0,2] with \\( \\int h(x)  dx = 1 \\) and \\( 0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1 \\). The previous solution used multi-resolution gradient-based optimization but was likely trapped in local optima and was slow due to the significant computation time required for correlation calculations. \n\nOur approach combines multi-resolution optimization with physics-informed basis functions and gradient projection to overcome these limitations. The core idea is to represent the step function \\( h(x) \\) as a superposition of localized basis functions, leveraging the fact that the optimal solution might be sparse in some sense. A hierarchical structure will allow balancing diversity and refinement, while analytical gradient calculations will provide efficient optimization.\n\n### Mathematical Specification\nThe step function \\( h(x) \\in [0,1] \\) on [0,2] is represented using piecewise constant basis functions, parameterized by overlapping \"atomic\" components:\n\n\\[\\boxed{h(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^N c_i \\cdot \\phi(x - x_i, \\sigma)}\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( \\phi(x - a, \\sigma) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma} \\Phi(\\lambda \\sigma^{-1} (x - a)) \\) is a scaled shifted function, \n- \\( \\Phi \\) is the \\(\\ell_1\\) pseudo-norm function \\(\\Phi(t) = \\sum_{k=-N_c}^{N_c} \\exp(-|k| \\cdot |t|) \\),\n- \\( c_i \\geq 0 \\), \\( \\sum_i c_i = M \\) (integral constraint), \n- \\( \\sigma = \\Delta \\) is a localization parameter.\n\nThis parameterization ensures:\n- Compact support for the basis functions (width \\( \\sigma^{-1} \\))\n- Adherence to \\( 0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1 \\) by thresholding\n- Efficient calculation of integral constraints\n\nThe objective is \\( \\boxed{C_5 = \\max_k \\left( \\frac{1}{2N} \\right) \\sum_{i,j} c_i c_j \\rho(\\bm{\\theta}) } \\) where \\( \\rho \\) calculates the correlation at shift \\( \\bm{\\theta} \\).\n\n### Implementation Procedure\n1. **Parent Handling**:\n   Initialize from `initial_h_values` or start randomly, ensuring \\(\\sum h(x) = 1\\). Use an interpolation-based transition algorithm between resolutions, dynamically adjusting the basis function parameters (\\(\\Delta\\)) while maintaining feasibility.\n\n2. **Basis Formation**:\n   Create localized basis functions \\( b_i(x) \\) with positions uniformly spaced over [0, 2] plus periodic repeats. Each basis function is truncated at its effective width \\(\\Lambda / \\Delta\\).\n\n3. **Sparse Population Initialization**:\n   Generate sparse non-zero coefficients in continuous \\(c_i\\) space, distributed with user-configurable concentration. Use hierarchical refinement: start wide and progressively narrow the spread of basis functions.\n\n4. **Layered Optimization**:\n   a. **Diffusion Phase**: Stochastic swaps between randomly selected basis nodes, followed by systematic constraint gradient projection.\n   b. **Amplification**: Periodic expansion of hyper-parameter space (\\( \\Delta \\) modifications) to explore broader search.\n\n5. **Termination Trigger**:\n   Stop when the minimum system \"temperature\" (\\( T_{\\text{init}} \\cdot e^{-4} \\)) or budget expires, or after satisfactory diversity loss.\n\n### Search Choices and Variations\n- **Population Configuration**:\n  - Start: Density parameter \\( N_0 = \\floor{(\\text{current scale})/3} \\)\n  - Local upgrade: \\( N = N_0 \\cdot \\min(2, \\text{local update factor}) \\)\n\n- **Transition criteria**:\n  Trigger resolution change when population diversity (quantified by centroid spread) drops below threshold.\n\n- **Objective calculation**:\n  - Interpolation length adjustment (systematic up-sampling)\n  - Careful gradient approximation using the Hessian-free approach for stability\n\n### Compute Budget and Robustness\nEstimate approximately 300 seconds/level (3 levels: coarse-to-fine). Each update evaluates 2 to 3 feasible solutions. Budget monitoring during evaluations ensures timely termination. Use Lipschitz constraints to bound variables.\n\n### Validation and Return Contract\n- Final solution must satisfy \\( 0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\) and \\( \\sum h_i = \\text{target} \\)\n- Cross-validated via reconstruction from basis functions and direct correlation\n- Hierarchical recording ensures fully evaluated solutions are captured",
